=========================================================
EM UNIT IN CSS
=========================================================

Definition:
em is a relative unit in CSS.
It is calculated based on the font size of the
PARENT element.

Unlike rem, em depends on its immediate parent.

---------------------------------------------------------
HOW EM WORKS
---------------------------------------------------------

Formula:
1em = parent element’s font size

Example meaning:
- Parent font size = 16px
- 1em = 16px
- 1.5em = 24px
- 0.75em = 12px

If the parent font size changes, em values change
automatically.

---------------------------------------------------------
EM FOR FONT SIZE
---------------------------------------------------------

Reference:
Font size in em is always relative to the parent’s
font size.

Key Points:
- Child elements inherit and multiply parent size
- Each nesting level recalculates em again
- This can lead to compounding

---------------------------------------------------------
NESTING & COMPOUNDING PROBLEM
---------------------------------------------------------

What is compounding?
When multiple nested elements use em, the size keeps
multiplying at each level.

Example meaning:
- Body font size = 16px
- Parent div = 1.25em → 20px
- Child p = 1.2em → 24px

This makes large layouts harder to control.

---------------------------------------------------------
EM FOR MARGIN & PADDING
---------------------------------------------------------

Reference:
When em is used for margin or padding, it is calculated
based on the element’s OWN font size.

This means:
- If font size increases, spacing increases
- If font size decreases, spacing decreases

This behavior is useful for proportional spacing.

---------------------------------------------------------
EM FOR WIDTH & HEIGHT
---------------------------------------------------------

Reference:
Width and height in em are calculated based on the
element’s font size.

Use Case:
- Buttons
- Badges
- Components that scale with text

---------------------------------------------------------
EM VS REM (QUICK COMPARISON)
---------------------------------------------------------

em:
- Relative to parent
- Can compound
- Context-based sizing
- Useful for component-level scaling

rem:
- Relative to root
- No compounding
- Global consistency
- Best for typography systems

---------------------------------------------------------
ADVANTAGES OF EM
---------------------------------------------------------

- Flexible and relative
- Scales naturally with parent
- Great for reusable components
- Maintains proportional spacing

---------------------------------------------------------
DISADVANTAGES OF EM
---------------------------------------------------------

- Harder to predict in deep nesting
- Compounding can cause issues
- More complex to debug

---------------------------------------------------------
WHEN TO USE EM
---------------------------------------------------------

Use em when:
- Building self-contained components
- Spacing should scale with text
- You want local control over sizing

Avoid em when:
- Building global typography systems
- Deep nesting exists
- Predictability is required

---------------------------------------------------------
IMPORTANT NOTE
---------------------------------------------------------

Best Practice:
- Use rem for global font sizes
- Use em for component-based spacing and sizing

Understanding em is essential for mastering
responsive and scalable CSS layouts.

---------------------------------------------------------
CORE RULE (MOST IMPORTANT)
---------------------------------------------------------

em does NOT behave the same for all properties.

font-size using em:
→ Depends on the PARENT element’s font size

margin / padding using em:
→ Depends on the ELEMENT’S OWN font size

---------------------------------------------------------
ELEMENT STRUCTURE USED FOR EXPLANATION
---------------------------------------------------------

div
 └── p
      └── ul

Given:
div font-size = 16px
p font-size   = 1em
ul font-size  = 2em

---------------------------------------------------------
FONT-SIZE CALCULATION (PARENT-RELATIVE)
---------------------------------------------------------

div:
font-size = 16px

p:
1em = parent (div) font size
1em = 16px × 1 = 16px

ul:
2em = parent (p) font size
2em = 16px × 2 = 32px

Final font sizes:
div = 16px
p   = 16px
ul  = 32px
